class Solution:
    def removeAlmostEqualCharacters(self, word: str) -> int:
        def are_almost_equal(a, b):
            return a == b or abs(ord(a) - ord(b)) == 1

        n = len(word)
        operations = 0
        i = 0

        while i < n - 1:
            if are_almost_equal(word[i], word[i + 1]):
                operations += 1
                # Skip the next character as it will be changed
                i += 2
            else:
                i += 1

        return operations
```

This solution iterates through the string and checks if adjacent characters are "almost-equal" (either the same or adjacent in the alphabet). If they are, it counts an operation and skips the next character (since changing it would resolve the conflict). This greedy approach ensures the minimum number of operations.

